{"id":"death-natural","name":"Death, Natural","aliases":["Death"],"sources":["torrey","easton"],"topics":["torrey:death-natural"],"groups":[],"definition":{"source":"easton","term":"Death","text":"May be simply defined as the termination of life. It is represented under a variety of aspects in Scripture: (1.) “The dust shall return to the earth as it was” (Eccl. 12:7). (2.) “Thou takest away their breath, they die” (Ps. 104:29). (3.) It is the dissolution of “our earthly house of this tabernacle” (2 Cor. 5:1); the “putting off this tabernacle” (2 Pet. 1:13, 14). (4.) Being “unclothed” (2 Cor. 5:3, 4). (5.) “Falling on sleep” (Ps. 76:5; Jer. 51:39; Acts 13:36; 2 Pet. 3:9. (6.) “I go whence I shall not return” (Job 10:21); “Make me to know mine end” (Ps. 39:4); “to depart” (Phil. 1:23). The grave is represented as “the gates of death” (Job 38:17; Ps. 9:13; 107:18). The gloomy silence of the grave is spoken of under the figure of the “shadow of death” (Jer. 2:6). Death is the effect of sin (Heb. 2:14), and not a “debt of nature.” It is but once (9:27), universal (Gen. 3:19), …"},"lemmas":[{"strong":"G2288","lemma":"θάνατος","xlit":"thánatos","gloss":"death","kjv":"X deadly, death","count":9,"share":0.16},{"strong":"G599","lemma":"ἀποθνήσκω","xlit":"apothnḗskō","gloss":"to die off","kjv":"be dead, death, die, lie a-dying","count":6,"share":0.11},{"strong":"G3498","lemma":"νεκρός","xlit":"nekrós","gloss":"dead","kjv":"dead","count":5,"share":0.09},{"strong":"G2198","lemma":"ζάω","xlit":"záō","gloss":"to live","kjv":"life, live, quick","count":4,"share":0.07},{"strong":"H4194","lemma":"מָוֶת","xlit":"mâveth","gloss":"death","kjv":"dead, death, die","count":4,"share":0.07},{"strong":"G386","lemma":"ἀνάστασις","xlit":"anástasis","gloss":"a standing up again","kjv":"raised to life again, resurrection, rise from the dead, that should rise","count":3,"share":0.05}],"verses":["Rom.5.12","Rom.6.9","1Cor.15.21","1Cor.15.26","2Tim.1.10","Heb.2.15","Heb.11.5","Rev.1.18","Rev.21.4",